This file is NOT a systemd-pcrlock component definition.

systemd-pcrlock(8) reads *.pcrlock and *.pcrlock.d/*.pcrlock from here
(man/systemd.pcrlock.xml). Nothing in this directory is one of those, and
deliberately so: everything a pcrlock definition can say about *this* machine is
per-machine or per-image, and a definition shipped in an image is fixed at build
time. The variant directories next to this file -- 400-secureboot-separator,
500-separator and 700-action-efi-exit-boot-services -- are systemd's, and they
ship empty on purpose: the per-machine values are generated at provisioning time
by the lock-* commands, which write into /var/lib/pcrlock.d (also searched, after
this one; upstream's own default set of component directories is

    /etc/pcrlock.d /run/pcrlock.d /var/lib/pcrlock.d
    /usr/local/lib/pcrlock.d /usr/lib/pcrlock.d

and later directories mask earlier ones only by filename, so the two compose).

WHAT RUNS, AND WHERE

  initrd, on the boot that provisions, before switch_root
      vngrd-tpm-pcrlock.service
        make-policy --components=/sysusr/usr/lib/pcrlock.d
                      --location=850-sysinit -> /sysroot/var/lib/systemd/pcrlock.json
      then systemd-cryptenroll --tpm2-pcrlock=... --tpm2-with-pin=yes

  host, every boot
      systemd-pcrlock-{firmware-code,firmware-config,secureboot-policy,
      secureboot-authority}.service   (upstream units, enabled as shipped)
      systemd-pcrlock-make-policy.service with
        --components=/usr/lib/pcrlock.d --location=850-sysinit (drop-in)
      systemd-pcrlock@.service and systemd-sysupdate-notify-pcrlock@.service,
        same drop-in, same reason
      vngrd-pcrlock-esp.service       re-writes the ESP copy every boot

The four lock-* units above DO run on the host, and that is not an oversight:
they keep /var/lib/pcrlock.d populated for the *host's* own use (systemd-pcrlock
log, an operator running systemd-pcrlock predict by hand). What they must never
do is reach the policy, which is why every make-policy path pins
--components= to a /usr/lib/pcrlock.d tree and not the default set that includes
/var/lib/pcrlock.d.

The two --components= paths are deliberately different and name the same files:
the initrd has no /usr/lib/pcrlock.d of its own (lib/initrd.sh seeds the initrd
from an allow list that never included it, and its assertion even forbids it --
so a bare /usr/lib/pcrlock.d in the enrollment loads NO components, silently,
and the policy comes out as PCR 15 alone), so it reads the verified /usr that is
already mounted at /sysusr/usr. The host's /usr/lib/pcrlock.d is that same tree
mounted over /. Same digests, so the two runs of make-policy agree.

lock-gpt, lock-kernel-initrd, lock-kernel-cmdline, lock-file-system and
lock-machine-id are not run anywhere. Their components land in a /var/lib/
pcrlock.d that the initrd cannot read, so they could only ever change the shape
of a policy no initrd can satisfy; see "WHAT THE POLICY COVERS" for the measured
consequence on PCR 5, and the file-system/machine-id note for PCR 15.

WHAT THE POLICY COVERS

On a machine that has booted twice, the enrollment's window (everything up to and
including 800-leave-initrd) requires these components to be present in the event
log, and the ones with definitions then make their PCRs predictable:

    PCR 0,1,2,3   firmware code and config        (lock-firmware-*)
    PCR 7         Secure Boot policy + authority  (lock-secureboot-*)
    PCR 13,14     the OS separator                (750-os-separator, shipped)

That is the measured set on a machine that has booted three times, from
`systemd-pcrlock --location=850-sysinit predict'. Each of those PCRs carries two
predicted alternatives (the two branches the component combinations produce), so
the policy is a set, not a single value -- which is what makes a firmware update
that only changes the *variant* (a new db entry no longer duplicated, say)
something the refresh can write.

Dropped, and why, all three reasons enumerated by event_log_reduce_to_safe_pcrs():

    PCR 4, 11, 12 the stub's own measurements into them have no component
                  definition, so the log holds unrecognized records. This is
                  also what keeps an A/B slot switch out of the policy: the
                  loaded image and the per-slot usrhash= are in these PCRs, and
                  they are exactly the PCRs nothing can predict.
    PCR 15        machine-id / root file system identity are measured on the
                  host, after the volume is open
    PCR 5         the GPT record is recognized (lock-gpt) but systemd-stub
                  also measures loader.conf into this PCR, and nothing maps
                  that record -- so the partitioning is not asserted on this
                  image, and lock-gpt is what makes it assertable. Measured:
                  "Event log record 29 (PCR 5, "systemd-boot: loader.conf")
                  not matching any component."
    PCR 9         never in the mask at all: DEFAULT_PCR_MASK starts at 0 and
                  skips 9, and the kernel's cmdline and initrd records live
                  there. (Which is the safe outcome for lock-kernel-cmdline:
                  see below.)

PCR 5's drop has nothing to do with the ordering of lock-gpt. On the boot that
provisions it drops *twice over* -- the GPT record in the log is the pre-repart
table, which the post-repart definition does not match -- and on every boot after
that it drops once, for the loader.conf record.

WHAT IS NOT HERE AND WHY

lock-machine-id: writes 820-machine-id.pcrlock, also PCR 15, and also into the
initrd's tmpfs at enrollment. It would do the same job as lock-file-system, but
the value it would name is the initrd's own randomly generated machine ID
(/etc/machine-id is regenerated on every initrd boot because the root's is
unreadable until the volume is open), so no policy could ever use it.

lock-kernel-cmdline (710) and lock-kernel-initrd (720): both claim PCR 9, and PCR 9
is where the kernel measures its own command line and initrd -- the measured log
shows "Linux: kernel command line" and "Linux: initrd" in it. So the records
exist, and a definition written from /proc/cmdline would map the first of them but
not the initrd; lock-kernel-initrd would need the exact cpio the kernel loaded,
which in a UKI is assembled by systemd-stub from several sections and is not a
file anyone can name. Defining either one would satisfy the cmdline and leave the
initrd unmapped, which changes nothing because the PCR is not in the default mask
anyway. Neither is run, here or on the host, and the A/B cmdline (per-slot
usrhash=) is the other reason not to: one variant per slot would be a list a
host-side unit cannot maintain, and a sysupdate would have to update the policy.

lock-uki / lock-pe: these need a path to the UKI and a --pcrlock= destination,
and the number of allowed values is bounded (tpm2_policy_super_pcr() refuses a
PCR with more than 8 predicted values). A policy over PCR 4 would have to name
every UKI any slot can ever boot, which is a list no host-side unit can maintain,
and it would make an A/B switch a policy change. PCR 4 is left unrecognized and
therefore unbound, which is also what lets a sysupdate move the image without
touching the root's seal.

Read ../systemd/system/systemd-repart.service.d/10-device.conf for what the root
volume is sealed to when there is no pcrlock policy yet -- on the boot that
provisions, the volume is opened by repart's own
PolicyAuthorize{PCR 11 signed} + PolicyPCR{7} token, and this enrollment is what
replaces it for every later boot.
